# Review: settings page (UX and accessibility)

- Scope: `public/settings.html`, `public/settings.css` (read-only review, no code changed)
- Date: 2026-11-11
- Method: static reading of the source. The page was not run in a browser, no screen reader or axe pass was done, and the `/settings` endpoint was not exercised. Runtime behaviour below is inferred from HTML/Fetch semantics. **Status: NOT VERIFIED at runtime.**
- Captures: 0 screenshots, 0 traces, 0 videos.

## Findings (most severe first)

| # | Severity | Where | Finding | Fix |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Blocker (functional) | html:11,12,16 | No field has a `name`, so `new FormData(form)` is empty. The POST sends no data, yet the page says "Saved". | Add `name="orgName"`, `name="currency"`, `name="voidAlerts"` (names must match what the server expects; confirm with backend). |
| 2 | Blocker (feedback) | html:22-23 | "Saved" is shown for any HTTP response, including 4xx/5xx, because `res.ok` is never checked. A network failure gives no message at all (no `.catch`). | Check `res.ok`; show a specific error on failure and on `.catch`; keep the user's input. |
| 3 | Blocker (a11y, WCAG 2.1.1) | html:17, css:3 | Save is a `<div onclick>`. It cannot be reached with Tab or triggered with Enter/Space, and has no button role. Keyboard and screen-reader users cannot save. | Use `<button type="submit">Save</button>` and handle the form `submit` event with `preventDefault()`. |
| 4 | High (functional) | html:10-11 | Pressing Enter in the name field triggers native implicit submission: the browser GETs the page with no data and reloads, bypassing `save()` and discarding input. | Fixed by the same `submit` handler as #3. |
| 5 | High (a11y, WCAG 1.3.1 / 4.1.2 / 3.3.2) | html:11,12,16 | No labels. The text input relies on a placeholder (disappears on typing, low contrast); the select has no accessible name; the checkbox text is not associated, so clicking it does not toggle the box. | `<label for="org-name">Organization name</label>`, a label for Currency, and wrap the checkbox: `<label><input type="checkbox" …> Email me when an invoice is voided</label>`. |
| 6 | High (a11y, WCAG 2.4.7) | css:4 | `*:focus { outline: none; }` removes all focus indicators. | Delete it; if styling is wanted use `:focus-visible` with a visible ring of ≥ 3:1 contrast. |
| 7 | High (a11y, WCAG 1.4.3) | css:1 | Body text `#999` on `#fff` is about 2.8:1, below the 4.5:1 minimum. | Use a darker text colour, e.g. `#595959` (≈ 7:1) or the design-system token. |
| 8 | High (a11y, WCAG 1.4.3) | css:3 | Button text `#eee` on `#ddd` is about 1.2:1, effectively invisible. | Use a dark-on-light or light-on-dark pair ≥ 4.5:1. |
| 9 | Medium (a11y, WCAG 4.1.3) | html:19 | `#status` is not a live region, so "Saved" or an error is not announced. | `<div id="status" role="status" aria-live="polite">` (use `role="alert"` for errors). |
| 10 | Medium (a11y, WCAG 1.3.1 / 2.4.6) | html:9 | The page title is a styled `<div>`, so there is no heading; screen-reader users cannot navigate by heading. No `<main>` landmark. | `<main><h1>Organization settings</h1> …</main>`. |
| 11 | Medium (UX) | whole page | Existing settings are never loaded, so the form opens blank and a save could overwrite real values with defaults (once #1 is fixed). No loading state. | Load current values before enabling the form; show a loading state, and an error state if loading fails. |
| 12 | Medium (UX) | html:17-24 | No in-progress state: the button stays active during the request, allowing double submission and giving no feedback on slow networks. | Disable the button and show "Saving…" while the request is pending. |
| 13 | Medium (UX) | html:11 | No validation; an empty organization name can be saved. Errors are not tied to the field. | Add `required` (if the name is mandatory — owner/backend to confirm) and use `aria-describedby` for field errors. |
| 14 | Low (responsive) | html:3-6 | No `<meta name="viewport">`, so mobile browsers render a zoomed-out desktop layout. Controls are also all inline with no layout. | Add `<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">`; stack fields with spacing; give targets ≥ 24×24 px (WCAG 2.5.8). |
| 15 | Low (UX) | html:5 | Generic `<title>Settings</title>`. | e.g. `Organization settings – <product>`. |
| 16 | Low (UX) | html:16 | "Email me" — whose e-mail address is used is not shown. | Show or link the destination address. |

## Outside this review's scope (flagged, not assessed)

- No CSRF token is visible in the POST; whether the server protects `/settings` was not checked. Route to a security review.
- Who may change organization settings (authorization) is not visible from the page.
- The currency options have no `value` attributes; this works (text is submitted) but should match the server's expected codes.

## Suggested minimal markup

```html
<main>
  <h1>Organization settings</h1>
  <form id="settings">
    <label for="org-name">Organization name</label>
    <input type="text" id="org-name" name="orgName" required>

    <label for="currency">Currency</label>
    <select id="currency" name="currency">
      <option value="EUR">EUR</option>
      <option value="USD">USD</option>
    </select>

    <label><input type="checkbox" id="void-alerts" name="voidAlerts"> Email me when an invoice is voided</label>

    <button type="submit">Save</button>
  </form>
  <div id="status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
</main>
<script>
  document.getElementById("settings").addEventListener("submit", async (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();
    const form = e.currentTarget, btn = form.querySelector("button"), status = document.getElementById("status");
    btn.disabled = true; status.textContent = "Saving…";
    try {
      const res = await fetch("/settings", { method: "POST", body: new FormData(form) });
      status.textContent = res.ok ? "Settings saved." : `Could not save settings (error ${res.status}).`;
    } catch {
      status.textContent = "Could not save settings. Check your connection and try again.";
    } finally {
      btn.disabled = false;
    }
  });
</script>
```

## Unverified

- Contrast ratios are computed from the hex values, not measured on screen.
- No assistive-technology, browser or mobile test was run.
- Server field names and response shape for `/settings` were not checked.
